        q: 'How would you handle a Kafka consumer that falls significantly behind (consumer lag)?',
        a: 'Steps: 1. Identify the lag with kafka-consumer-groups.sh or monitoring tools. 2. Scale out consumers (if partitions allow). 3. Optimize consumer processing (batch DB writes, async operations). 4. Check for slow downstream systems. 5. Increase fetch.max.bytes and max.poll.records for throughput. 6. If permanently behind, reset offsets and replay from a checkpoint.',
        detailedAnswer: 'Consumer lag = partition offset - consumer committed offset. Monitoring: `kafka-consumer-groups.sh --describe --group my-group`. Alert threshold: typically lag > 10,000 messages or growing trend. Root causes: (1) slow DB writes — batch inserts instead of row-by-row, (2) downstream API rate limits — add backpressure, (3) message processing is CPU-intensive — scale out consumers or optimize code, (4) data spike — add more partitions (requires coordination). Last resort: reset consumer group offset to latest to skip the backlog (accept data loss).',
