        a: 'Use a Lakehouse for Spark-first engineering on Delta tables in OneLake and a Warehouse for SQL-first serving, full DML, views, and stored procedures. Many senior Fabric designs use both: Lakehouse for Bronze and Silver transforms, Warehouse for Gold serving or analyst-owned SQL.',
        detailedAnswer: 'A Lakehouse exposes Delta data through a read-only SQL Analytics Endpoint; writes happen through Spark notebooks or pipelines. A Warehouse is a managed T-SQL engine with INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, MERGE, stored procedures, and stronger fit for SQL-heavy teams. Choose Lakehouse when you need medallion engineering, time travel, and Direct Lake over Delta. Choose Warehouse when analysts or DBAs need a familiar SQL surface, strong concurrency, and T-SQL-first deployment patterns. Governance is similar at the platform level, but the operational model differs: Lakehouse encourages file and layout tuning, while Warehouse pushes query design and semantic model discipline. Cost/performance-wise, Lakehouse is usually cheaper for engineering-heavy transformations, while Warehouse should not become the raw-ingestion landing zone. Common mistake: forcing every workload into one store instead of matching the store to the team and the grain.',
