        q: 'How would you implement row-level security in Fabric so that analysts only see their own region\'s data?',
        a: 'Layer it at the data layer, not just the report layer: Entra ID security groups per region, Fabric Warehouse RLS or Power BI dynamic RLS for row filtering, and OneLake data access roles where folder-level Lakehouse access is needed. Never rely on report-only security if users can reach the SQL endpoint directly.',
        detailedAnswer: 'Implementation usually starts with Entra groups such as APAC-Analysts, EMEA-Analysts, and NA-Analysts so onboarding is a group assignment, not an individual permission chase. Then apply Warehouse RLS or semantic-model RLS based on the reporting surface: Warehouse RLS protects direct SQL access, while Power BI RLS protects report consumers. If analysts also need notebook access, add OneLake data access roles to restrict which folders they can read. Auditing should come from Purview and Fabric logs so you can show who accessed which region and when. The tradeoff is that stronger security usually means more setup up front, but it is much safer than relying on separate ad hoc permissions in multiple places. Cost/performance-wise, RLS should be filtered on a selective column and tested at scale so the security layer does not become a query bottleneck. Common mistake: implementing RLS only in Power BI and assuming the underlying data is safe.',
        followUps: ['What is the difference between Fabric workspace RBAC and OneLake Data Access Roles?', 'How do you audit data access in Fabric?', 'What happens if RLS is only applied at the Power BI layer?'],
