        q: 'Fabric Spark job is processing 2TB of Delta files daily and running out of memory. How do you diagnose and fix it?',
        a: 'Diagnose: check Spark UI for stage-level data skew (one executor processing 50% of data while others are idle), look for OOM errors in driver vs. executor logs, check for cartesian joins or large broadcast joins. Fix: repartition on a high-cardinality column to eliminate skew, enable AQE (spark.sql.adaptive.enabled=true), replace broadcast joins with sort-merge joins for large tables, use Delta OPTIMIZE to reduce small file overhead before reading.',
        detailedAnswer: 'Skew diagnosis: `df.groupBy("partition_col").count().orderBy(desc("count"))` — if top partition is 10x average, you have skew. Fix: `df.repartition(200, "customer_id")` or use AQE skew join optimization (auto in Databricks Runtime 10+). Memory diagnosis: Driver OOM means you called .collect() or toPandas() on a large result — fix by keeping results in DataFrames. Executor OOM means a partition is too large — reduce partition size with repartition or salt the skewed key. Delta optimization before processing: `OPTIMIZE silver.orders ZORDER BY (customer_id)` then read — eliminates thousands of small files that each require a task, reducing scheduler overhead. Fabric Spark autoscales workers but cannot fix logic-level problems like skew or unnecessary shuffles.',
