        q: 'How would you build a real-time CDC (Change Data Capture) pipeline from a production database to a data lake?',
        a: 'Use Debezium (open-source) or cloud-native CDC (AWS DMS, Azure Database Migration Service) to capture row-level changes from the source database transaction log. Stream changes via Kafka topics (one per table). Consumer writes changes to Delta Lake using MERGE (upserts) — guaranteeing idempotency. Result: a near-real-time replica of the source database in the lake without impacting source system performance.',
        detailedAnswer: 'Debezium setup: connects to MySQL binlog, PostgreSQL WAL, or SQL Server CDC. Each change event: `{ op: "u", before: {id:1, status:"pending"}, after: {id:1, status:"shipped"}, ts_ms: 1705000000 }`. Kafka consumer reads events, applies MERGE on Delta Lake: `MERGE INTO orders USING staged ON orders.id = staged.id WHEN MATCHED AND op = "d" THEN DELETE WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET ... WHEN NOT MATCHED AND op != "d" THEN INSERT ...`. OPTIMIZE + ZORDER after each batch to maintain read performance. Lag monitoring: Kafka consumer lag < 30 seconds to qualify as "real-time".',
